I recorded P2.T1 as "ready-for-agent, fully specified" an hour ago. That was
premature: I had checked the FFI contract and the struct layout but not whether the
field types can actually be BUILT. Measuring that changes the item's size class.

`[]String` is fine — tallow_array_len is declared (codegen.rs:1401), the count-slot
convention is real, and "StringArray" is a consumed kind with .len routing
(emit_array_count_inline :7609, emit_array_count_value :14082).

`Map` is not. The layout registers field_kinds["open_graph"] = "Map" (codegen.rs:1946)
and a Map does lower to I8Ptr (:20736), but nothing in codegen ever PRODUCES a Map
value: rg for '%struct.Map|map_alloc|MapEntry' in codegen.rs returns zero matches,
and the "Map" tag has no consumer — unlike "StringArray", which has the routing above.
The interpreter, by contrast, returns a real Value::Map from extract_open_graph
(stdlib/browser.rs:73) and asserts it (:1430).

So P2.T1's step 2 ("a serialized string for open_graph") contradicts the kind its own
layout registers. That decides whether this item is a binding (small, as scoped) or a
language feature (Map values in AOT, far beyond a scraper task), and it is a decision
rather than a task. Both options and a recommendation are recorded; the FFI module is
deliberately NOT written, because a function nothing can call correctly is the
uncalled-helper anti-pattern this plan exists to remove.
